Z||000005||Patrick||06-03-2000||12:13 AM||||<BR>Is your cooling system in need of a major burp? <P>If you've got a lot of air in there it's not going to cool properly, no matter if you've taken the thermostat out and have the fan running continuously.<P>The proper way to (re)fill a Fiero cooling system has been covered here numerous times.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroSTETZ||06-03-2000||01:35 AM||||well, what i did was put it in my driveway, nose downhill, have the front and back caps off, then pour in 50/50 mix until it comes out the front, then capped it up, ran for a min, then repeated. is this right? I also was told by the fieroholic at my muffler shop that sometimes the thermostat housing cap can leak air in and cause cooling problems- is this true? can my overheating problems actually be caused by a problem with my heads? is there any way to check? sorry for all the ?'s, but until i get my new job, my income is less than 400/mo.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Linx||06-03-2000||02:21 AM||||How about your temperature sensor?  Not the one for the gauge...the one for the ECM?   The ECM turns on the fan.<P>Have you ever cleaned your radiator?  I once had overheating problems, and after replacing sensors, thermostats and caps...I discovered the radiator clogged with debris.  Im talking the <I>exterior</I>; no air could flow through it!  It went undetected because the A.C. condenser is in front of it and makes a good inspection impossible.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||06-03-2000||02:38 AM||||<BR>After you put the cap back on the radiator, did you continue to fill the system until the coolant came out the top of the thermostat housing?<P>Do you have the proper amount of coolant in the overflow tank beside the radiator?||||reg||5
